  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{37}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=36.$",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=36,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(36)$ and ${f}^{′}(36)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(36)=\\sqrt{36}=6,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(36)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x)=6+\\frac{1}{12}(x-36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{37}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=37.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{37} & ≈L(37) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}(37-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
